{"step_index":97,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-09T15:12:02Z","thinking":"**Re-evaluating Export Feedback**\n\nThe prompt requires addressing ongoing reports of incomplete or slow ledger exports. Reviewing existing documentation within the product workspace is the starting point to understand the current state and identify potential next steps. Given the context of the read-only snapshot, the focus will be on assessing implemented solutions and identifying potential areas of improvement or further investigation.\n\n\n**Analyzing Ledger Export History**\n\nInitial investigation reveals `Ledger Ferry` (deployed 2026-06-24, `record-c0268a81ef`) was retired under `data-portability` due to limited benchmarking (10,000 rows, `record-e617de6a9e`), and no tests with NDJSON or 1M+ workspaces (`record-1a7eb091b1`). No evidence directly addresses the current reports of incomplete or slow exports.\n\nSince prior work was limited in scope, current reports may stem from issues beyond initial benchmarks. The absence of comprehensive testing introduces uncertainty regarding Ledger Ferry's performance with larger datasets or different data formats, as suggested in reports.\n\nRecommendation:\n\n```\n# Dated Recommendation: 2026-09-09\n\n## Context:\nReports of incomplete/slow ledger exports. Investigating history of Ledger Ferry.\n\n## Prior Actions:\n- Ledger Ferry deployed 2026-06-24 (record-c0268a81ef).\n- Ferry retired under data portability.\n- Limited benchmarked at 10,000 rows (record-e617de6a9e).\n- Description correction 2026-09-07 noted only 10,000 row test and no NDJSON/1M+ workspace testing (record-1a7eb091b1).\n\n## Evidence:\nLimited testing of Ledger Ferry likely does not encompass the range of reported export issues, based on available history.\n\n## Next Action:\nPrioritize investigation of current export behavior with larger datasets and NDJSON formats. Assess the impact of workspace size.\n\n## Uncertainty:\nExact cause of current reported incomplete/slow exports remains unknown. Data portability retirement may have had unintended consequences on export performance with various ledger configurations.\n\n```\n/workspac\n<truncated 3619 bytes>\ns the hypothesis of a TTL issue.\n\nTicket analysis reinforces that both row loss and 403 errors are common failure points. There's a clear connection between the reported issues and limitations in the existing export process.\n\n\n**Analyzing Additional Export Data**\n\nNew data `record-6a8741fe3b` confirms NDJSON exports exceeding 2M rows (2,039,441 produced) with an HTTP 200 status, indicating completion, but raises a cursor checkpoint warning, suggesting possible data inconsistencies. This contrasts with earlier issues of row loss.\n\nFurther investigation required, as the cursor checkpoint warning signals potential issues. The dashboard correction audit `record-d46f9580f1` on September 7th covers smaller CSV jobs (<100k rows) and may not apply to this data.\n\nThe unknown submission date of `record-f10fee8e5d` remains, despite its import on September 7, which adds uncertainty to its context and how it relates to recent issues.\n\n\n**Summarizing Identified Export Issues**\n\nThe analysis confirms two primary issues: expired URLs causing 403 errors on long-running CSV exports (`record-f10fee8e5d`, `record-eb2ad86fcb.json`), and row loss during cursor checkpoint resumption in large exports (`record-339725ec76`, `record-6a8741fe3b.json`). The definition of `age_days` requires clarification as shown in `current.md` vs `record-b851997b7c.json`.\n\nPrevious verification efforts have been inadequate, indicated by `record-d46f9580f1.md`, `record-1a7eb091b1.md`, `record-e617de6a9e.md`, and `record-7c21733985.md`. Suggested technical fixes include deferring signed URL generation or extending TTL, and resolving cursor resumption bugs in account relation scans. Automated tests with 1M+ and 2M+ row fixtures and checkpoint-interruption scenarios 